      "band": "high_school_grades_9_12",
      "subject": "algebra",
      "prompt": "Solve 3(2x - 5) = 27. State x and show the essential steps.",
      "output": "To solve the equation \\(3(2x - 5) = 27\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Distribute the 3 on the left side of the equation:**\n   \\[\n   3(2x - 5) = 6x - 15\n   \\]\n   So, the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   6x - 15 = 27\n   \\]\n\n2. **Add 15 to both sides of the equation:**\n   This will isolate the term with \\(x\\) on one side.\n   \\[\n   6x - 15 + 15 = 27 + 15\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   6x = 42\n   \\]\n\n3. **Divide both sides by 6:**\n   This will isolate \\(x\\).\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{42}{6}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the right side gives:\n   \\[\n   x = 7\n   \\]\n\nSo, the solution is \\(x = 7\\).",
